A||||23||K9||HELP!!! Can anyone in this world fix my stearing poblem?||||8
Z||000000||K9||09-16-1999||08:59 AM||||At high speeds my 85 GT unstable (sloppy) stearing. It follows the waves in the road.Also when breaking the car has a slight pull to the left.I have done everything to fix this but the problem still remains.I have done the following:<BR>-Jacked up the front and looked for play<BR>-Replaced the stearing damper shock<BR>-Replaced the stabliser bar bushings<BR>-four wheel alaiment<BR>-had two different shops look at it<BR> (no cause found)<BR>-replaced wheel bearings<BR>Can anyone help or is anyone hveing the same problem? ||||reg||8
Z||000001||ChadMan||09-16-1999||09:50 AM||||When you replaced the wheel bearings, how tight did you tighten them down?  I replaced mine, and thought I had tightened them down properly, but as the new bearings seated themselves, I noticed my car getting rather drifty and wandery.  The bearings needed further tightening.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||09-16-1999||11:11 AM||||K9, My car has the exact same problem and aligning the wheels helped a bit, but not much. Changing to stiffer tires (tyres) will also help a lot. Anyway, I'm curious about other people's suggestions.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-16-1999||11:46 AM||||bad shocks and/or struts can also cause this.<P>If all the steering gear is in good shape, then try a better tire. Mine wandered quite a bit on the last set of rubber I had... The new ones only wander when I go over certain metal deck bridges in the area.||||reg||4
Z||000004||lowCG||09-16-1999||12:41 PM||||Yeah,cheap shocks,springs,and tires will make a car,that we all have in our minds to be an exotic,handle more like a chevette.<BR>Was looking at some more expensive cars last week,and they all had one thing in common;the lowest part of the airdam was around 4" from the ground.I may try this soon,just to see if there's anything to be gained on our cars.||||reg||3
Z||000005||Dale||09-16-1999||01:47 PM||||You should notice a conciderable difference with the addition of an air dam.  <P>K9,  you mentioned it was at high speeds when the car wonders.  these cars have a tendancy to float at high speeds due to the amount of air going under the car.  This is also further inhanced by evrything mentioned above.  It scares me to see people going to fast without the proper set-ups.<P>Back in 85 a friend was killed becouse of this (and lack of skill).||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||09-16-1999||03:15 PM||||I want to get my car setup as close to my old car as possible,then try and get some track time with some of the local clubs in order to gain some further driving skills.It was very predictable,easy to drive at the limit.<BR>I think for anyone who is serious about handling,that the coilover kits are most likely the way to go,with raised spindles,or taller balljoints to get the tie-rods up,even with the rack(reduces bump-steer).<BR>All my efforts have basically duplicated the coilover kits,taken a lot of time and craftwork to(yet)complete as well.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||squid||09-16-1999||08:01 PM||||If your rear suspension is sloppy, it will cause the problems you described. Taller sidewall rear tires will cause it too. If the rear is floating back and forth, youd have to keep changing your steering input.||||reg||1
Z||000008||David||09-16-1999||09:20 PM||||My '86 would dart from side to side while going down the freeway at about 60+ mph (100kph).  Tightened the front wheel bearings and it is much better.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||09-17-1999||12:12 AM||||Replace the bearings,and it'll get even better.Usually the cage gets sloppy/worn.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||09-17-1999||12:43 AM||||Worn ball joints and tired control bushings (front and rear) can also be partly to blame. It's probably a combination of several things.||||reg||1
Z||000011||PaUL||09-17-1999||12:46 AM||||Well heres my guess, could be a loose tie rod. Check the tierod ends with the front 2 wheels off the ground. Try moving the wheels by hand, there should be no movement at the tierod pivot point. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||K9||09-17-1999||08:44 AM||||Well I have done all you people said.I jacked up bouth sides of the front and had my dad shaked the tires while I looked under the car for play. No play found, any other ideas.<BR>  ||||reg||9
Z||000013||ChadMan||09-17-1999||09:27 AM||||Hey, Paul, I've done this "shake" test on my 86 SE, and there seems to be no movement at the pivot end, but there is some noticeable play at the other end (where the tie rod enters the bellows on the steering rack).  Is this normal?  If not, is this most likely a bad steering rack or bad tie rod ends?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Howard||09-17-1999||12:41 PM||||I had the same problem with mine, did everything that was memtioned too. then 1 day i was working on the back end and noticed<BR>that one bolt for the engine cradle was broken right off. When that was fixed,another<BR>4 wheel alignment was needed , it now drives like a brand NEW CAR.<p>[This message has been edited by Howard (edited 09-17-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Eddie||09-17-1999||08:18 PM||||Hay K9<BR>did you take your car to see if it was ever hit. You may have a bent frame. Space frame <BR>when hit are almost unfixable<P><BR>					GOOD LUCK Eddie||||reg||4
Z||000016||Taijiguy||09-18-1999||08:40 PM||||Try bumping your tire pressure up some, and make sure there's not alot of drifference from front to back..(some is acceptable, and even may be desirable depending on setup) Another thing touched on, but not directly addressed is if there's any free play in the steering wheel......if you have more than a tiny amount of free play in the steering wheel, you may need to adjust preload on steering box, or effect some repairs there. Good luck!||||reg||4
Z||000017||Harry||09-21-1999||08:52 PM||||Definitly look into inner tie rod ends.<BR>Also watch the shop whos doing you alignment.<BR>If they make any changes to your suspension with the car jacked up, after they set it down the cars suspension does not allow it to settle out all the way and after you start to drive it the alignment is way off.<BR>I had to different shops pull this one on me even said they set it so it would not pull on the left lane of the freeway.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Harry||09-21-1999||08:54 PM||||My fix to this was to just set the toe my self making very small adjustments at a time, watching just how much my steering wheel changes and going with the feel of it till it's right. I suppose the best way would be to pull it off the rack then pull it right back up again and check it all over again.||||reg||1
Z||000019||lowCG||09-21-1999||09:16 PM||||That car company from Italy,Ferrari,I believe,has a tolerance of 20 minutes of a degree,with most of it's suspension allignment settings,where our cars are only to the degree;get the picture,if the tolerance is tighter,at least it;ll make it within the degree.Our cars may get set a degree or two off,only because the unit of measure has been enlarged.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||87GT||09-21-1999||09:22 PM||||All I got to say is that my car has gotten worse since I bought it.(swaying) This morning, on my way to work, I was going down the road and started around a curve and felt like the whole rear of the car wanted to come around.  I was only doing 40 mph.  Im going to take it to my cousin who has done all the mechanic work on this car.  Its gotta be fixed.  Scared the hell out of me this morning.  8-/  Shocks and Struts have already been replaced. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||JetroGT||09-22-1999||12:45 AM||||My SE had a bad front end problem. When I test drove it I noticed that the alignment was way off. I got it alinged right away after I bought it. The alignement lasted about two weeks. It pulled when stoping and I had a bad vibration around 65-70 Mph. After a bit of messin' with it I found out that the rack was bad. It got rid of the pull I felt in the wheel when stoping, no more vibration at highway speed, and the rattle went away too. The first place quoted me $1000. to replace the rack. I told them "Get my car down, I'm outta hear". Another place did it for about $350. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||goldpan||09-22-1999||09:54 AM||||When I replaced my rear tires on my 86SE with Big O's HXP I had the high speed wonders.  I thought the bands where off center.  Went back to Big O, they said to lower the air pressure from 44psi to 32psi.  That fixed my problem.||||reg||1
Z||000023||WGraffiKs||09-22-1999||05:31 PM||||K9, Jack up the car on one side at a time and try to detect movement of the rear wheel from front-to-back (in and out).  If the wheel moves at all, remove the wheel and check the tightness of the link that connects to the axle assembly (I'm sorry, I don't know what is't called)  It looks like a tie rod end, and fits through the rear assembly, fastens with a big nut & cotterpin. When it gets loose, the rear alignment is off because it allows the rear wheel to change (caster or toe-in?), especially when cornering.  A good clue is it only does it (badly) cornering in one direction.  The taper at the tie-rod or the mount itself may be worn.  I'd check BOTH, even if the first side you check seems loose.  I had an 87 with this problem, after tightening and an alignment, it fixed the symptoms. ||||reg||1
